<p>News out of Dudley: <strong>W.W. Wood Products has rolled out a new ProCore veneer platform for <a href="/brands/eclipse-cabinetry">Eclipse Cabinetry</a>, and it brings back veneered finished ends on Eclipse plywood stain orders.</strong> If you've been quoting Eclipse plywood and explaining away a particleboard or MDF finished end that didn't quite match the exterior, that conversation is over for wood (stain) finishes. The transition is already done — every applicable plywood order has been moved onto the new platform.</p>

<p>Here's what changed, what didn't, and what's still coming.</p>

<h2>What changed: veneered ProCore plywood for finished ends on stain orders</h2>

<p>Wood (stain) finishes with the standard Maple interior now include <strong>veneered ProCore plywood for finished ends, wall tops, bottoms, and utility tops</strong>. That means the exposed exterior surface of the box is now a real wood veneer matched to the door species — no more integrated particleboard or MDF where a finished end ought to be.</p>

<p>This is a meaningful upgrade for designers selling Eclipse into projects with island returns, peninsula ends, tall exposed sides, and any elevation where the cabinet box edge is part of the architecture. It is also one less footnote in the proposal.</p>

<h2>The 12 species available with ProCore veneer</h2>

<ul>
  <li>Alder</li>
  <li>Cherry</li>
  <li>Hickory</li>
  <li>Maple</li>
  <li>Poplar</li>
  <li>Quarter Sawn White Oak</li>
  <li>Reconstituted Walnut</li>
  <li>Reconstituted White Oak</li>
  <li>Red Oak</li>
  <li>Rift Cut White Oak</li>
  <li>Walnut</li>
  <li>White Oak</li>
</ul>

<h2>Pricing: 10% plywood upcharge is unchanged</h2>

<p><strong>The plywood upcharge on Eclipse stays at 10%</strong> — the veneer-finished-end upgrade is rolled in at the same number. No new line item, no separate finished-end charge to find on the price list. If your customer was already willing to pay the 10% to move from particleboard to plywood construction, they are now also getting the veneered finished ends as part of that decision.</p>

<h2>What's still Partial Plywood: Acrylics, HPLs, Paint, PVs, and TFLs</h2>

<p>When ordered as plywood, the following finishes <strong>continue to be Partial Plywood</strong>:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Acrylics</li>
  <li>HPLs</li>
  <li>Paint</li>
  <li>PVs (polymer veneer / polymer laminate)</li>
  <li>TFLs</li>
</ul>

<p>Partial Plywood means the box is plywood but the <strong>finished ends are integrated particleboard or MDF</strong>. <strong>Wall top and bottom panels remain plywood and will match the exterior finish to the interior.</strong> Worth saying out loud to dealers: nothing got worse here. Partial Plywood is the same product these finishes have been built on. The change is that <em>stain</em> just got better.</p>

<h2>Coming next: paint on ProCore</h2>

<p>The announcement from W.W. Wood includes one explicit hint about what's next:</p>

<blockquote>Paint will be available on ProCore plywood in the future. We will make an official announcement about the new ProCore platform once we have the paint available on ProCore, until then please inform your accounts.</blockquote>

<p>Translation: paint-on-ProCore is on the roadmap, and there will be a formal launch when it lands. For now, paint orders run on Partial Plywood as described above. Pinnacle Sales will push the update out as soon as it's released.</p>

<h2>How to talk about this with your designers and clients</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Eclipse plywood + stain just got better at no extra cost.</strong> If a project is in design with veneered or stained Eclipse and the client elected plywood, the spec already includes veneered finished ends. No reorder, no change order — the factory is already building it that way.</li>
  <li><strong>Use it as a closer on stain projects on the fence about plywood.</strong> The 10% upcharge now buys both the plywood construction <em>and</em> the veneered finished ends. The value math is better than it was a month ago.</li>
  <li><strong>For paint projects, set expectations correctly.</strong> Paint on Eclipse plywood today = Partial Plywood = integrated particleboard or MDF on the finished ends. That is still the right answer right now. When paint-on-ProCore lands, we'll re-quote on request.</li>
  <li><strong>Wall cabinet bottoms and tops still match exterior to interior</strong> on Partial Plywood orders. That part of the spec was already correct and hasn't changed.</li>
</ul>

